
Network Traffic Monitoring & Detection Lab

Overview

I built a small network security monitoring lab using Ubuntu Server and Ubuntu Desktop to understand how network traffic can be captured, filtered, analyzed and detected.

The main tools I worked with were:
	•	Suricata
	•	Zeek
	•	tshark
	•	tmux
	•	curl

I used the Ubuntu Server as my monitoring machine and Ubuntu Desktop as the machine generating test network traffic.

The purpose of the project was to get practical experience with network monitoring and to understand how a SOC Analyst can use network telemetry to identify suspicious activity.

Lab Environment

Ubuntu Server

Used as the monitoring and security analysis machine.

Tools installed/configured:
	•	Suricata
	•	Zeek
	•	tshark
	•	tmux

Ubuntu Desktop

Used to generate normal and test web traffic.

I used curl from the Ubuntu Desktop to create HTTP requests that could then be observed and analyzed on the Ubuntu Server.

Basic Lab Setup

The general flow of the lab was:

Ubuntu Desktop
     |
     |  Network traffic
     |  curl requests
     v
Ubuntu Server
     |
     +---- tshark
     |
     +---- Suricata
     |
     +---- Zeek
     |
     +---- tmux

This allowed me to generate traffic from one machine and monitor that traffic from another machine.

1. Capturing Network Traffic with tshark

I first used tshark to observe network packets moving through the Ubuntu Server.

This helped me understand that network communication is made up of individual packets containing information such as:
	•	Source IP
	•	Destination IP
	•	Source port
	•	Destination port
	•	Protocol
	•	Packet details

I also learned how to capture traffic into a .pcapng file so that it could be analyzed later.

Example workflow:

tshark -i <interface> -w capture.pcapng

The important lesson for me was that packet capture gives me the raw network evidence, while tools such as Zeek and Suricata can help turn that traffic into more useful security information.

2. Using curl to Generate Test Traffic

From the Ubuntu Desktop, I used curl to generate HTTP traffic.

For example:

curl http://<server-ip>

This gave me a simple way to create traffic that I could observe from the Ubuntu Server.

Using curl helped me understand the relationship between:

Client request
      ↓
Network packets
      ↓
Traffic capture
      ↓
Security monitoring

Instead of just looking at traffic generated by normal applications, I could deliberately create traffic and then investigate what happened.

3. Using tmux for Monitoring

I used tmux to keep multiple monitoring sessions running in the same terminal.

For example, I could have one tmux pane showing packet capture while another pane was being used for Suricata or Zeek analysis.

This was useful because I did not have to repeatedly close and reopen terminal sessions.

A simplified view of how I used it was:

+----------------------+----------------------+
|                      |                      |
|      tshark          |      Suricata        |
|   traffic capture    |       alerts         |
|                      |                      |
+----------------------+----------------------+
|                                             |
|                  Zeek                       |
|             log analysis                   |
|                                             |
+---------------------------------------------+

This gave me a better understanding of how analysts can monitor several pieces of security telemetry at the same time.

4. Suricata

I used Suricata as a network intrusion detection tool.

Suricata examines network traffic and can generate alerts when traffic matches security rules.

I worked with Suricata rules and tested traffic against them.

Some of the rules I worked with were related to:
	•	HTTP traffic
	•	SYN scanning
	•	Port 445 activity

I also learned about rule SIDs and encountered a duplicate SID issue while configuring a custom rule.

That troubleshooting experience was useful because it showed me that writing a detection rule is not just about creating the rule itself. The configuration also needs to be valid so that Suricata can load the rule successfully.

5. Testing Suricata Detection

After configuring the rules, I generated traffic from the Ubuntu Desktop and monitored the Ubuntu Server for Suricata alerts.

The basic process was:

Generate traffic
      ↓
Suricata inspects traffic
      ↓
Traffic matches a rule
      ↓
Alert is generated
      ↓
SOC Analyst investigates

This helped me understand how an IDS can turn network traffic into security alerts.

6. Zeek

I also used Zeek to analyze captured network traffic.

One of the things I learned is that Zeek works differently from a traditional packet capture tool.

Instead of only looking at individual packets, Zeek creates useful logs describing network activity.

For example:

conn.log
dns.log

conn.log

I learned that conn.log provides information about network connections, including details such as:
	•	Source
	•	Destination
	•	Ports
	•	Protocol
	•	Connection information

dns.log

I learned that dns.log provides information about DNS activity.

This can be useful when investigating suspicious domains or unusual DNS behavior.

7. Analyzing a PCAP with Zeek

I used a captured network file and processed it with Zeek.

Example:

/opt/zeek/bin/zeek -r capture.pcapng

Zeek then generated logs that I could investigate.

This helped me understand the difference between:

PCAP
↓
Raw packet evidence

Zeek logs
↓
Structured network activity

The structured logs are easier to search and investigate when looking for suspicious behavior.

8. Suricata vs Zeek vs tshark

One of the most important things I learned was that these tools serve different purposes.

Tool	What I used it for
tshark	Capturing and inspecting packets
Suricata	Detecting suspicious traffic using rules
Zeek	Creating structured network security logs
tmux	Managing multiple monitoring sessions
curl	Generating test HTTP traffic

I learned that these tools can complement each other rather than replacing one another.

9. Example SOC Workflow

The lab helped me understand a simple SOC workflow:

Traffic generated
       ↓
Packet captured
       ↓
Network traffic analyzed
       ↓
Suricata detects suspicious activity
       ↓
Zeek provides additional network context
       ↓
Analyst investigates the event
       ↓
Determine whether activity is
benign or suspicious

For example, if Suricata generates an alert, I would not immediately assume that an attack occurred.

I would investigate:
	•	Source IP
	•	Destination IP
	•	Source port
	•	Destination port
	•	Protocol
	•	Time of activity
	•	Related Zeek logs
	•	Whether the traffic was expected

This helped me understand the difference between receiving an alert and investigating an alert.

10. What I Learned About Network Traffic

Before working on this lab, I mostly understood network traffic from a theoretical perspective.

Working with tshark, Suricata and Zeek helped me see the actual relationship between network activity and security monitoring.

I learned that:
	•	Network traffic can be captured as packet data.
	•	Packets contain information that can be investigated.
	•	IDS tools can detect activity based on rules.
	•	Network monitoring tools can produce structured logs.
	•	DNS activity can provide useful investigation clues.
	•	A single alert may need additional context before deciding whether it is malicious.

11. Troubleshooting Experience

I also encountered configuration issues while building the lab.

One example was a duplicate Suricata rule SID.

I had to identify the problem and correct the custom rule configuration before Suricata could use the rules properly.

I also had to work with network interfaces and traffic capture settings to make sure the monitoring machine could actually see the traffic I was generating.

These problems were useful because they gave me experience troubleshooting the tools instead of only following installation instructions.

12. Practical SOC Investigation Example

Scenario

A Suricata alert is generated after network traffic is sent from the Ubuntu Desktop.

Investigation

I would investigate:

1. What traffic generated the alert?
2. Which source IP generated it?
3. Which destination was contacted?
4. What protocol and port were involved?
5. Does Zeek show the same connection?
6. Was the activity expected?
7. Is this a true positive or false positive?

Evidence

I can correlate information from:

Suricata alerts
       +
Zeek logs
       +
tshark packet capture

This gives me more context than relying on a single alert.

